<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Chhota Udaipur State</h1><p class="st-meta">1743 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Chhota Udaipur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Chauhan Rajput state of eastern Gujarat whose rulers traced their line to the medieval kings of Pavagadh and Champaner.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-begada">Mahmud Begada</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champaner">Champaner</a> in 1484, members of the Khichi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhan</a> ruling family fled east and founded the states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhota-udaipur">Chhota Udaipur</a> and Baria. Chhota <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a> emerged as a separate state around 1743.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The state lay in forested hill country with a largely Adivasi population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathwa">Rathwa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhils">Bhil</a> and other communities. It paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaekwads">Gaekwads</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/baroda-state">Baroda State</a> and later belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rewa-kantha-agency">Rewa Kantha Agency</a>, ruled by a Maharawal. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">Timber</a> and forest produce were important; the Rathwa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pithora-painting">Pithora paintings</a> come from this area.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After accession to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhota-udaipur">Chhota Udaipur</a> was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a> in 1948 and became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in 1960, now as Chhota <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a> district.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chhota-udaipur-state.jpg" alt="Chhota Udaipur State" width="200" height="134"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Nataraja at French Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Drapeau_Chota_Udaipur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1743 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chhota Udaipur</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism; Adivasi traditions</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gujarati, Rathwi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chhota Udepur</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1846&amp;focus=chhota-udaipur-state" aria-label="Chhota Udaipur State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6662 1353 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chhota Udaipur State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6982 1609L7364 1601M6982 1609L7364 1602M6982 1609L7018 1386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7018 1386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6982 1609h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6982 1609h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1846&amp;focus=chhota-udaipur-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
